{"chunk_id": "a0cb842e43c2", "text": "This section continues Bhishma\u2019s instruction to Yudhishthira on ethics, wisdom, and liberation. It first teaches moral discipline: the wise restrain their senses, endure insult without retaliation, avoid anger in religious acts, and cultivate truth, compassion, patience, and renunciation. Truth is declared the supreme sacred principle and the sure path to heaven. Association shapes character, and the gods favor those devoted to righteousness rather than sensory indulgence. The dialogue then shifts to philosophical inquiry about Sankhya and Yoga. Bhishma explains that both systems aim at emancipation and share virtues such as purity, vows, and compassion, differing mainly in method\u2014Yoga relying on disciplined practice and Sankhya on knowledge grounded in scripture. He elaborates the power of Yoga: through self-control, concentration, and ascetic discipline, a yogin breaks worldly bonds and attains liberation.\nBhishma contrasts Sankhya and Yoga paths to liberation and describes the discipline, strength, and practices through which Yogins attain emancipation.\nThemes: Sankhya vs Yoga philosophy, self-restraint and truth, ignorance and attachment, yogic discipline, path to emancipation", "vector": [0.01180938072502613, 0.004044580738991499, 0.024042416363954544, -0.012186698615550995, -0.024386636912822723, 0.024942683055996895, -0.015119184739887714, -0.021910902112722397, -0.014338071458041668, -0.032330166548490524, 0.008625349029898643, 0.025392817333340645, 0.023764392361044884, 0.01694619655609131, -0.015423687174916267, 0.001232074573636055, 0.055392879992723465, -0.003471984528005123, 0.029020361602306366, -0.012716267257928848, 0.0027438276447355747, -0.00906224362552166, -0.01039278507232666, -0.0027620315086096525, -0.012557396665215492, 0.009942651726305485, 0.03770528733730316, -0.023142149671912193, -0.008102400228381157, -0.025988580659031868, -0.010889255441725254, -0.0010425882646813989, -0.00396845489740